# Type Systems For The Uninitiated

Since the recent [^chatgpt] boom of AI assisted programming, there's been a steadily increasing interest about programming languages, specially around type systems. If you are even remotelly present in Twitter/X, Youtube or HackerNews you've seen many projects being rewritten to more "type safe" languages like [Rust](https://rust-lang.org/) or work being done to strenght guarantees in languages like [Typescript]() and even [new languages](https://x.com/kitlangton/status/2119137520529375499) with heavy focus on types.  


The running hypothesis is clearly stated: *Type Systems make Agentic Engineering better and easier*.

But *why?* What even _is_ a type system and what does it do?

> This post is written aiming at people who are comfortable with the basic ideas of programming or even have had professional experience, in which case you are already using all the concepts introduced, even if unaware.

## Introduction

I've have always been drawn to programming languages, ever since first learning to code by writing little C exercises, just the notion of having all these little verbs and names make the computer do something was incredible to me. Six years forward, after learning what it takes for that to happen, from microcode to syscalls, the amazement hasn't really wane. I truly believe that learning about them is a crucial part of developing a literacy on the field.

I, like many of you, have not had a _formal_ education on programming languages. I've essentially did what most out there do, just messed around until all the concepts and jargon you can see being thrown around everywhere online start making sense, and the first thing I did was exposing myself to languages that force you to [_think_](https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/). Because of that, many concepts will not be explained formally because that's not the audience this is meant for.

## Type Systems

To start talking about type systems we have to first talk about what is a type. Since this is can go a lot of different ways lets adopt the nomenclature of the _"[Type Systems](https://web.archive.org/web/20260917195400/https://courses.grainger.illinois.edu/cs421/fa2018/CS421A/resources/cardelli.pdf)"_ paper by Luca Cardelli and say that:

> "A program variable can assume a range of values during the execution of a program. An upper bound of such a range is called a *type* of the variable."  

For example, you can be sure that in a *typed language* a variable explictily or implicitly[^milner] declared as 16-bit signed will only range from the values $`-2^{15}` to $`2^{15}-1` throught the program execution because there is a component, the type system, which has the job of rejecting any program that breaks the _type rules_ by making _judgements_ on the types in a program, it essentially tries very hard to prevent execution errors.

### Safe languages

Before, I stated that there is a trend of moving to "safer" languages, I intently use quotes around safer because it's important to understand that type safety[^safety] is defined as the property of a program which has ruled out any errors that silently corrupt the program, which Cardelli names _unexpected errors_. But what each language chooses to consider as an unexpected errors can vary, so that's why they can be compared as less or more safe in respect to each other.

 Cardelli often uses the term _forbidden errors_ for a set formed by all the unexpected and a subset of the trapped errors (errors which stop the program immediately, think like trying to divide a number by zero) and states that a program that doesn't cause forbidden errors is *type sound*.

Languages try to enforce these behaviours by relying on static and runtime checks during the program execution, independently that they are typed or untyped, think about every compile time error you've faced in Typescript or every runtime check error in Python.

It's important to emphasize again that *language safety is a binary property*, you are either completely safe or you are not. Because of that, the actual useful part of the dicussion is how many errors can be prevented, *how much of the potential space of programs written in that language are type safe*.

### Preservation and Progress

It's not trivial to prove type soundness in a program. Even if the language has a reduced set of keywords and is really careful on the features it adds, language design is a combinatorial problem and the evaluation of a type sound program, by definition, can't get stuck at any time. Getting stuck means that during the execution of the program, it can never reach a point where no evaluation rule in the language applies, if that happens we are talking about a expression that should have been rejected by the type system. This is where the theorems of Preservation and Progress are applied. 

First, let's introduce some notation to express these theorems precisely:

| Symbol | Meaning | Example |
|:--|:--|:--|
| $`\Gamma` | Typing context. It maps variables to their types and carries all assumptions currently in scope. | $`\Gamma = \{x : \mathrm{Int},\ f : \mathrm{Int} \to \mathrm{Bool}\}` |
| $`\vdash` | Turnstile operator. Read as "under the assumption of". Separates the context from the typing conclusion. | $`\Gamma \vdash e : \tau` — in context $`\Gamma`, $`e` has type $`\tau` |
| $`\tau` | Stands for any concrete type in a judgment. | $`\vdash 42 : \tau` where $`\tau = \mathrm{Int}` |
| $`:` | Typing relation. It relates an expression to its type. | $`\mathrm{true} : \mathrm{Bool}` — true has type Bool |
| $`\rightarrow` | Single-step evaluation. It is one reduction step in the semantics of the language. | $`1 + 1 \rightarrow 2` |
| $`\mathrm{val}` | Value predicate. Denotes that an expression is a value and cannot reduce further. | $`e\ \mathrm{val}` means $`e` is a value |
| $`\land` | Logical "and". Both propositions on either side must hold simultaneously. | $`e\ \mathrm{val} \land e : \tau` |
| $`\lor` | Logical "or". At least one of the propositions must hold. | $`\tau = \mathrm{Int} \lor \tau = \mathrm{Bool}` |
| $`\varnothing` | Empty typing context. No variable assumptions are in scope. | $`\varnothing \vdash 5 : \mathrm{Int}` is a closed term |
| $`\exists` | Existential quantifier. Witnesses some value or type satisfying a given property. | $`\exists \tau.\ \Gamma \vdash e : \tau` |
| $`.` | Binder operator. Read as "such that". | $`\exists x.\ P(x)` — there exists $`x` such that $`P(x)` |

Where $`Γ` represents all the variables that are in the scope alongside their types. It's used in the relation $`Γ ⊢ e : τ` that can be read as "the typing context _shows_ that $`e` has type $`τ`".

*Preservation* can be expressed as:

$$`\Gamma \vdash e : \tau \land e \rightarrow e' \Longrightarrow \Gamma \vdash e' : \tau`


Preservation can be read as _"if under the typing context $`Γ`, $`e` has type $`τ`, and $`e` steps to $`e'` in one step, then $`e'` also has type $`τ` under $`Γ`"_. It ensures that each step of evaluation preserves the type of the variable, remember what we talked about ranges before.

and *Progress* as:

$$`\varnothing \vdash e : \tau \Longrightarrow (e\ \mathrm{val}) \lor (\exists\ e'.\ e \rightarrow e')`

Progress and can be read as _"if under the empty typing context $`∅`, $`e` has type $`τ` then $`e` is either a value or there exists an expression $`e'` reachable by a single step from $`e`"_. It ensures that well-typed closed expressions (essentialy a whole type sound program) are either values or can be further evaluated and will not get stuck. 

## What about untyped languages?

Also referred to as "Dynamic Languages", they are languages that embrace a model of computation where they do not have any types whatsoever or multiple _classes_ of values exist for a single recursive type. Quoting Robert Harper's Practical Foundations for Programming Languages: 

> _"Every dynamic language is inherently a static language in which we confine ourselves to a (needlessly) restricted type discipline to ensure safety."_

Here every value is _tagged_ to indicate which _class_ of value they belong through a process called _Classification_. Since the only static check done is that the expression is well-formed, meaning it parsed correctly, and that there are _no free variables_ in the expression; Checks for errors that would have never show up in a statically-typed language are done at runtime by a process called _Class Checking_ using the tags each value was given.


This changes how the theorems are proven. Progress is expressed as:

$$`d\ \mathrm{ok} \Longrightarrow (d\ \mathrm{val}) \lor (d\ \mathrm{err}) \lor (\exists\ d'.\ d \rightarrow d')`

Progress now reads as "If $`d` is a closed expression, then it's either a value, results in a runtime error or it can be further evaluated".

And there's now a  _Exclusivity_ lemma:

$$`\forall\ d : \text{exactly one of } (d\ \mathrm{val}),\ (d\ \mathrm{err}),\ (\exists\ d'.\ d \rightarrow d')`

Which states that, for any $`d` in the language, _exactly one_ of these outcomes must be true at any given time.

In the previous model, these errors were guaranteed not to happen! But, since catching those errors before evaluation requires static typing, now they have become part of the behaviour (the semantics) of the language! Since there exists only one singular recursive type, _Preservation_ plays lesser of a role here, there is no static type information to preserve between evaluations! 

*** 

It's useful to look at an example in the wild. This single recursive type idea can be found in the [CPython Language Reference](https://docs.python.org/3/), where it defines a type called [`PyObject`](https://docs.python.org/3/c-api/structures.html#c.PyObject), where *all object types are extensions of this type* and one of its fields, `ob_type`, is a pointer to a [`PyTypeObject`](https://docs.python.org/3/c-api/type.html#c.PyTypeObject) that encodes _the object type_ which is exactly the idea of tagging a value to know which class of value they belong to.

### Subtyping

Subtyping is essentially a way to extend a type system, by treating types as a collection of values, by the *subsumption principle*:

> If $`τ'` is a subtype of $`τ`, then a value of type $`τ'` may be provided whenever a value of type $`τ` is required.

This relation essentially enlarges the set of well-typed programs, allowing the type system to consider values of one type to be treated as values of another. [^complex]

[^complex]: Since an expression doesn't fully reveal the information about the underlying value, proving safety is more delicate and the proofs of preservation and progress change but we'll skip showing them.

This will sound familiar if you read about the [SOLID](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SOLID) principles, since [Barbara Liskov's substitution principle](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liskov_substitution_principle) is indeed one definition of this subtyping relation and it's a really common feature in OOP languages.

To understand subtyping, is useful to think in matters of _behaviors_. We'll use $`T <: S`  to show that $`T` is a subtype of $`S`.

To work some quick examples, in languages with nominal typing like Java, nominal essentialy means that types have an identity that makes them unique, one often needs to declare the relation explicitly:

``` Java
class Spren {
    void fly() {}
}

class Bridgeman {
   void fly() {}
  
}

// We explicitly declare Herald as subtype of Spren.
// Herald <: Spren
class Herald extends Spren {
  void fly() { }

  void do_nothing() {}
}

// This works
Spren Jezrien = new Herald();

// Bridgeman has `fly()` but because we don't declare it
// as a subtype of Spren, it will fail to compile.
Spren Rock = new Bridgeman();

```

For comparison, TypeScript uses structural typing, where types are identified by their definition[^javascript], so we can do things that would've been illegal in Java:

[^javascript]: because it's meant to have backwards compatibility around JavaScript, where anonymous objects are what is commonly used.

``` TypeScript
type Bridgeman = {
  run(): void;
};

type Herald = {
  fly(): void;
  do_nothing(): void;
  run(): void;
};

const jezrien: Herald = {
  fly() {},
  do_nothing() {},
  run() {},
};

const run = (b: Bridgeman) => {
  b.run();
};

// This works because subtyping is based solely on the type's members.
run(jezrien);

// This also works because the point of having structural typing in TS
// is to still be able to write JavaScript.
run({
  run() {},
});

```

This example can look awfully similar to duck typing in Python, but it is not. Structural subtyping in typescript is verified statically, while in duck typing there is no subtyping relation taking place, the program will attempt to dispatch the required method function at runtime, and will fail if it isn't there: [^static-duck]

``` Python
from io import BytesIO

class FakeFile:
    def __init__(self, content: bytes, name: str):
        self._buf = BytesIO(content)
        self.name = name

    def seek(self, pos):
        self._buf.seek(pos)


# I don't really care what file_like is, as long as it has a `read()` method.
def save_file(file_like, destination: str):
    with open(destination, "wb") as out:
        out.write(file_like.read())


with open("real.txt", "wb") as f:
    f.write(b"i am a real file")

with open("real.txt", "rb") as real:
    save_file(real, "out_real.txt")

fake = FakeFile(b"i am a duck", "duck.txt")
save_file(fake, "out_duck.txt")

```

``` fishshell
$ uv run main.py

Traceback (most recent call last):
  File "/home/lautaro/Documents/Development/pypypy/main.py", line 25,
 in <module>
    save_file(fake, "out_duck.txt")
  File "/home/lautaro/Documents/Development/pypypy/main.py", line 15, in
 save_file
    out.write(file_like.read())
            ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
AttributeError: 'FakeFile' object has no attribute 'read'

```

[^static-duck]: Python actually supports structural subtyping (named static duck-typing) in the shape of `protocols`. [Check PEP 544](https://peps.python.org/pep-0544/)

## Conclusion

This is it! There are still a lot of things I didn't cover, but I focused on those I've had experience with (that's why I don't give more examples using dynamic languages!).

I'm sure there are more interesting applications of types from languages like [Haskell](https://www.haskell.org/), [Elixir](https://elixir-lang.org/), and Lisps like [Clojure](https://clojure.org/) but I haven't used them.

If you want to keep learning how to use types to write efficient software I can't recommend enough to read the archive of [Ted Kaminski](https://www.tedinski.com/archive/), especially "_[The One Ring Problem](https://www.tedinski.com/2018/01/30/the-one-ring-problem-abstraction-and-power.html)_"! 

